% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 5 "Negation as Failure", section 5.5
%         ("Moore's older-brother example, the selective closed-world assumption").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% Book: "I do not have an older brother if I cannot show that I have an older
% brother." In LE the "cannot show" is negation as failure. The closed-world
% assumption is applied selectively to "has an older brother": absence of proof
% means the negative holds. So that the rule has people to range over, the
% individuals are introduced with "is a person" facts; the rule quantifies over
% those people and concludes the negative for anyone with no positive fact.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a person* is a person.
*a person* has an older brother.
*a person* does not have an older brother.

the knowledge base older_brother includes:

a person does not have an older brother
    if the person is a person
    and it is not the case that
        the person has an older brother.

scenario robert_and_john is:
    robert is a person.
    john is a person.
    john has an older brother.
    no_brother expects answers ["robert does not have an older brother"].

query no_brother is:
    which person does not have an older brother.
